\title{Phantom Topologies and the Splitting of Reality:\\
A Lattice-Theoretic Theory of Observer-Dependent Spaces}
\author{Aristotle}
\date{2026-07-03}

\begin{document}
\maketitle

\begin{abstract}
We introduce and study \emph{phantom topologies}: structures in which the
topology of a fixed set is not absolute but depends on an observer, and the
``real'' topology is defined as the unanimous agreement of all observers.
Formally, a phantom topology on a set $X$ is a family $T=(T_i)_{i\in\iota}$ of
topologies on $X$, and its \emph{consensus} topology is the supremum
$\bigsqcup_i T_i$ in the lattice of topologies---the finest topology all
observers agree is open. We recast the classical refinement lattice as a theory
of observers and consensus, and identify \emph{splittability}
(join-reducibility) as the precise invariant governing when a reality can be
distributed across genuinely sharper viewpoints. Our main results are: (i) a
two-observer decomposition of the Euclidean real line as the consensus of its
lower-limit and upper-limit topologies; (ii) an \emph{Indiscrete Splitting
Theorem} stating that on every set with at least two points the indiscrete
topology is the consensus of two strictly finer ``co-excluded-point''
observers, each resolving exactly one deleted point; (iii) a resulting genuine
two-observer representation of every $\ge 2$-point blurred space; and (iv) an
\emph{Extremal Dichotomy}: the indiscrete topology is always splittable while
the discrete topology is always rigid. Splittability is thus an
order-theoretic property, orthogonal to cardinality and separation strength:
maximally blurred reality is never irreducibly blurred, whereas maximally sharp
reality can never be reassembled from sharper views.
\end{abstract}

\section{Introduction}

Classical topology fixes a single notion of nearness on a space. Yet
measurement in physics, partial information in computation, and competing
sensory channels all suggest that nearness might be relative to an observer,
with objective structure emerging only where observers agree. This paper
develops a rigorous model of that intuition and extracts from it a sharp
structural theorem.

The technical backbone is classical: the topologies on a fixed set $X$ form a
complete lattice under \emph{refinement}. We adopt the convention that $t\le s$
means $t$ is \emph{finer} than $s$. Then the discrete topology $\bot$ (all
subsets open) is the bottom element, the indiscrete topology $\top$ (only
$\emptyset$ and $X$ open) is the top element, and the supremum of a family is
the topology whose open sets are exactly those open in every member.

Our contribution is a reframing---families of topologies as observers, and
their supremum as consensus---together with a theorem: the maximally blurred
topology always decomposes into two strictly sharper observers, via an
explicit minimal construction, in sharp contrast to the rigidity of the
maximally sharp topology.

\section{Preliminaries: the lattice of topologies}

Let $X$ be a set. A \emph{topology} on $X$ is a family
$\tau\subseteq\mathcal P(X)$ of \emph{open} sets with $\emptyset,X\in\tau$,
closed under arbitrary unions and finite intersections.

For topologies $s,t$, write $t\le s$ iff every $s$-open set is $t$-open ($t$ is
finer). This makes the topologies on $X$ a complete lattice with minimum
$\bot=\mathcal P(X)$ (discrete), maximum $\top=\{\emptyset,X\}$ (indiscrete),
and supremum $\bigsqcup_i T_i$ whose open sets are $\bigcap_i T_i$.

\begin{lemma}[Agreement characterization]\label{lem:agree}
For any family $(T_i)_{i\in\iota}$ of topologies on $X$ and any $U\subseteq X$,
\[ U\in\bigsqcup_i T_i \iff U\in T_i \text{ for all } i. \]
\end{lemma}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
The right-hand side defines a topology (an intersection of topologies is a
topology), is coarser than each $T_i$, and contains every topology coarser than
all $T_i$; hence it is the least upper bound.
\end{proof}

\section{The phantom framework}

\begin{definition}[Phantom topology]
A \emph{phantom topology} on $X$ with observer set $\iota$ is a function
$T:\iota\to\{\text{topologies on }X\}$, assigning observer $i$ the topology
$T_i$.
\end{definition}

\begin{definition}[Consensus]
The \emph{consensus} topology of $T$ is
$\consensus(T):=\bigsqcup_{i\in\iota}T_i$, the finest topology all observers
agree on. A set is consensus-open iff it is open for every observer
(Lemma~\ref{lem:agree}).
\end{definition}

\begin{proposition}[Measurement coarsens]\label{prop:coarsen}
Each observer is finer than the consensus: $T_i\le\consensus(T)$ for all $i$.
Hence enlarging the observer set can only coarsen the consensus.
\end{proposition}

\begin{proof}
$T_i\le\bigsqcup_j T_j$ is the defining property of the supremum.
\end{proof}

\begin{definition}[Splittable / rigid]\label{def:split}
A topology $\tau$ on $X$ is \emph{splittable} (\emph{join-reducible}) if there
exist $a,b$ with $a<\tau$, $b<\tau$, and $a\sqcup b=\tau$; otherwise $\tau$ is
\emph{rigid}. A splittable topology is exactly one admitting a \emph{genuine}
two-observer phantom representation: a phantom topology with consensus $\tau$,
each of whose two observers is strictly finer than $\tau$.
\end{definition}

\section{The real line as a two-observer consensus}

\begin{definition}
The \emph{lower-limit} observer $L$ declares $U$ open iff every $x\in U$ admits
$b>x$ with $[x,b)\subseteq U$. The \emph{upper-limit} observer $Up$ declares
$U$ open iff every $x\in U$ admits $a<x$ with $(a,x]\subseteq U$. Both are
topologies on $\mathbb R$.
\end{definition}

\begin{theorem}[Two-observer line]\label{thm:line}
The consensus of the lower-limit and upper-limit observers is the standard
Euclidean topology on $\mathbb R$: $L\sqcup Up=\tau_{\mathrm{std}}$. Neither
observer alone equals $\tau_{\mathrm{std}}$, and the two are distinct, so the
representation is genuinely phantom with exactly two observers.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
A Euclidean-open set satisfies both interval conditions, hence is
consensus-open. Conversely, if $U$ is open for both observers, each $x\in U$
has $[x,b)\subseteq U$ and $(a,x]\subseteq U$, whose union $(a,b)\ni x$ lies
in $U$, a two-sided neighborhood. The witnesses $[0,1)$ (lower-open, not
Euclidean) and $(0,1]$ (upper-open, not Euclidean) show distinctness.
\end{proof}

\section{The Indiscrete Splitting Theorem}

\begin{definition}[Co-excluded-point topology]
For $a\in X$, the \emph{co-excluded-point topology} $\coExcl(a)$ has open sets
exactly $\{\emptyset,\ X,\ X\setminus\{a\}\}$.
\end{definition}

\begin{lemma}
$\coExcl(a)$ is a topology.
\end{lemma}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
$\emptyset,X$ are open. Every pairwise intersection among the three sets is
again one of them, and any union is $X$ (if it contains $X$),
$X\setminus\{a\}$ (if it contains that but not $X$), or $\emptyset$.
\end{proof}

\begin{lemma}[Strict refinement]\label{lem:strict}
If $|X|\ge 2$ then $\coExcl(a)<\top$ for every $a$.
\end{lemma}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
$\coExcl(a)\le\top$ always; and $X\setminus\{a\}$ is $\coExcl(a)$-open but not
indiscrete-open, being nonempty (some $b\ne a$ exists) yet omitting $a$.
\end{proof}

\begin{lemma}[Join of two deleters]\label{lem:join}
For distinct $p\ne q$, $\ \coExcl(p)\sqcup\coExcl(q)=\top.$
\end{lemma}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
By Lemma~\ref{lem:agree}, $U$ is open in the join iff
$U\in\{\emptyset,X,X\setminus\{p\}\}$ and $U\in\{\emptyset,X,X\setminus\{q\}\}$.
Since $p\ne q$, we have $X\setminus\{p\}\ne X\setminus\{q\}$ and neither equals
$\emptyset$ or $X$; the only common members are $\emptyset$ and $X$, i.e.
$\top$.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Indiscrete Splitting Theorem]\label{thm:split}
For every set $X$ with $|X|\ge 2$, the indiscrete topology is splittable:
there exist $a,b$ with $a<\top$, $b<\top$, $a\sqcup b=\top$. Explicitly, for
any $p\ne q$, the pair $(\coExcl(p),\coExcl(q))$ works.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
Pick distinct $p,q\in X$. By Lemma~\ref{lem:strict} both $\coExcl(p)$ and
$\coExcl(q)$ are strictly finer than $\top$; by Lemma~\ref{lem:join} their join
is $\top$.
\end{proof}

\section{Genuine representation and the Extremal Dichotomy}

\begin{corollary}[Genuine two-observer representation]
Every $X$ with $|X|\ge 2$, under the indiscrete topology, admits a genuine
finite phantom representation with two observers $T_0=\coExcl(p)$,
$T_1=\coExcl(q)$ ($p\ne q$): the consensus is $\top$ and each observer is
strictly finer than $\top$.
\end{corollary}

\begin{proof}
Immediate from Theorem~\ref{thm:split} and Definition~\ref{def:split}.
\end{proof}

\begin{lemma}[Discrete rigidity]\label{lem:rigid}
The discrete topology $\bot$ is rigid: it is not $a\sqcup b$ with $a<\bot$ and
$b<\bot$.
\end{lemma}

\begin{proof}
$\bot$ is the lattice minimum, so no topology is strictly finer than $\bot$;
no such $a$ exists.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Extremal Dichotomy]\label{thm:dichotomy}
On any $X$ with $|X|\ge 2$, the indiscrete topology $\top$ is splittable
(Theorem~\ref{thm:split}) while the discrete topology $\bot$ is rigid
(Lemma~\ref{lem:rigid}).
\end{theorem}

\begin{remark}
The dichotomy refutes ``more open sets $\Rightarrow$ easier to split.'' The
discrete topology has the most open sets, yet is the canonical rigid space,
because rigidity is about position in the lattice, not the size of the
topology. Splitting requires strictly finer topologies to serve as observers;
the discrete topology, at the bottom, has none.
\end{remark}

\section{Computation on finite spaces}

On finite $X$ the topologies form a finite lattice, so splittability is
decidable. One represents a topology as a family of bitmasks containing
$\emptyset,X$ and closed under union and intersection; tests splittability of a
target $\tau$ by searching pairs of strictly-finer topologies whose common
open sets equal $\tau$; and computes consensus by intersecting open-set
families (Lemma~\ref{lem:agree}). These confirm the Extremal Dichotomy
computationally and census the rigid (join-irreducible) topologies.

\section{Discussion and future work}

\paragraph{Census of rigid finite spaces.} Among the four topologies on a
two-point set, exactly two---the discrete topology and each Sierpi\'nski
topology---are rigid, while the indiscrete splits. We conjecture that on an
$n$-point space the rigid topologies are precisely the join-irreducible
elements of the lattice, counted by a recurrence tied to covers directly below
$\top$.

\paragraph{The cofinite topology.} On an infinite set, declare $U$ open iff it
is empty or omits finitely many points. We conjecture this reality is rigid,
admitting no finite multi-observer representation---a genuinely infinite rigid
space.

\paragraph{Dense orders.} We conjecture that for every dense linear order
without endpoints, the order topology is exactly the consensus of its
lower-limit and upper-limit observers, so every such order has phantom number
exactly two.

\section{Conclusion}

We recast the lattice of topologies as a theory of observers and consensus, and
proved that maximally blurred reality always splits into two sharper viewpoints
(each deleting one point), while maximally sharp reality is rigid.
Splittability is an order-theoretic invariant, orthogonal to size and
separation---reality as the invariant core of many views, made mathematically
precise.

\end{document}

